    "scenario_title": "Unexpected Fracture of the L-92 Structural Alloy",
    "scenario_description": "A newly developed high-strength structural alloy, designated L-92, has shown promising mechanical properties in laboratory testing but has exhibited unexpected fracture failures when deployed in high-load industrial components. These failures occur inconsistently, with some components surviving under identical nominal conditions while others fracture prematurely.\n\nMaterials engineers suspect that subtle interactions between processing parameters and in-service conditions may be responsible. Prior work on similar alloys suggests that heat treatment and cooling profiles influence precipitate formation and residual stress, while trace impurities at grain boundaries can strongly amplify these effects. However, the relative importance of these factors, and which interventions would most effectively reduce failure risk, remain unclear.",
    "domain": "materials science",
    "theoretical_context": "Previous studies on precipitation-hardened alloys indicate that excessive precipitate density can embrittle grain boundaries, especially in the presence of impurity segregation. Other work emphasizes residual stress as a dominant driver of crack initiation under cyclic or high loads. There is debate in the field about whether optimizing heat treatment alone is sufficient, or whether controlling operating conditions and load profiles is equally critical."
  },
